Leeloo-ma-tu-pass.

1284
00:54:35.360 --> 00:54:36.320
What does that mean?

1285
00:54:36.540 --> 00:54:39.520
Are you saying her last name or are you saying Leeloo has a multi-pass?

1286
00:54:39.660 --> 00:54:40.680
Like she's going to a-

1287
00:54:40.680 --> 00:54:45.080
She's going to outer space and you have to have this multi-pass to get on this ship.

1288
00:54:45.120 --> 00:54:46.140
Is it like a metro card?

1289
00:54:46.440 --> 00:54:47.060
Kind of.

1290
00:54:47.360 --> 00:54:48.480
Oh, you have to see this.

1291
00:54:48.580 --> 00:54:49.680
Is Bruce Willis in it?

1292
00:54:50.160 --> 00:54:51.260
I do love Bruce Willis.

1293
00:54:51.320 --> 00:54:52.440
Chris Tucker's in it.

1294
00:54:52.580 --> 00:54:56.080
But this Leeloo costume was basically white gauze.

1295
00:54:56.180 --> 00:54:56.900
So much skin.

1296
00:54:56.900 --> 00:55:00.120
Yes, well you have to see it because she's in like this.

1297
00:55:00.260 --> 00:55:01.800
Oh God, I don't even know how to explain it.

1298
00:55:01.940 --> 00:55:03.640
But she is The Fifth Element.

1299
00:55:04.240 --> 00:55:04.460
Okay.

1300
00:55:04.860 --> 00:55:05.260
Leeloo.

1301
00:55:05.800 --> 00:55:06.080
All right.

1302
00:55:06.360 --> 00:55:08.380
Okay, so I guessed Mindy Kaling and I was right.

1303
00:55:08.540 --> 00:55:09.160
Mindy Kaling.

1304
00:55:13.480 --> 00:55:17.820
I want to talk about one of my personal favorite episodes of The Office, which also happens

1305
00:55:17.820 --> 00:55:20.280
to be our very first Halloween episode.

1306
00:55:20.480 --> 00:55:20.600
Okay.

1307
00:55:20.820 --> 00:55:23.880
It aired during season two and was called, appropriately, Halloween.

1308
00:55:24.020 --> 00:55:26.080
I cry at the end every time I watch it.

1309
00:55:26.360 --> 00:55:28.280
So the plot is very simple if you don't remember.

1310
00:55:28.280 --> 00:55:32.100
However, Michael is told that he must fire someone during the month of October due to

1311
00:55:32.100 --> 00:55:33.320
cutbacks at Dunder Mifflin.

1312
00:55:33.500 --> 00:55:38.240
But in true Michael fashion, he waits until the very last day of October, which also happens

1313
00:55:38.240 --> 00:55:38.880
to be Halloween.

1314
00:55:39.360 --> 00:55:43.660
Greg Daniels wrote this episode and I remember him telling me that the inspiration for the

1315
00:55:43.660 --> 00:55:47.740
episode was that he had always wanted to do a story about someone being fired, but

1316
00:55:47.740 --> 00:55:50.460
from the point of view of the boss rather than the employee.

1317
00:55:50.780 --> 00:55:55.320
He said, we always see stories of the people being fired, but rarely stories about the person

1318
00:55:55.320 --> 00:55:56.400
having to do the firing.

1319
00:55:56.400 --> 00:55:59.880
And I always thought that Greg's decision to set the story on Halloween when everyone

1320
00:55:59.880 --> 00:56:04.520
is dressed up and excited for our office Halloween party made the whole thing even more delightfully

1321
00:56:04.520 --> 00:56:05.600
uncomfortable to watch.

1322
00:56:05.860 --> 00:56:06.580
Oh, so tragic.

1323
00:56:06.880 --> 00:56:10.480
Like no one wants to get fired when they're dressed up like a clown or something.

1324
00:56:10.620 --> 00:56:10.680
Exactly.

1325
00:56:10.680 --> 00:56:11.220
It's depressing.

1326
00:56:11.700 --> 00:56:16.420
But the one problem that this plot presented for Greg was that he would actually have to

1327
00:56:16.420 --> 00:56:18.060
fire someone on the show.

1328
00:56:18.140 --> 00:56:22.400
And he didn't want to fire any of the core cast members, even though many of the supporting

1329
00:56:22.400 --> 00:56:25.120
characters at this time were still under weekly contracts.

1330
00:56:25.120 --> 00:56:28.180
You guys were not considered series regulars yet.

1331
00:56:28.340 --> 00:56:29.180
Oh no, not at all.

1332
00:56:29.440 --> 00:56:33.480
You know how you say you have a photo of the exact moment you knew we'd be best friends?

1333
00:56:33.720 --> 00:56:33.900
Yes.

1334
00:56:34.020 --> 00:56:38.520
I have a photo of the exact moment I was made series regular and you and I are jumping up

1335
00:56:38.520 --> 00:56:43.640
and down, giddy, holding hands, jumping up and down because I got the news while we were

1336
00:56:43.640 --> 00:56:44.600
on booze cruise.

1337
00:56:45.000 --> 00:56:46.580
That's when we became series regulars.

1338
00:56:46.580 --> 00:56:48.720
But this was before booze cruise.

1339
00:56:48.980 --> 00:56:49.280
Yes.

1340
00:56:49.500 --> 00:56:53.300
So he could have any of us fired any of you guys, but he didn't want to.

1341
00:56:53.500 --> 00:56:59.620
But he also didn't want to hire a new person just for one episode, establish them and fire

1342
00:56:59.620 --> 00:57:01.160
them because it felt dishoneSt.

1343
00:57:01.260 --> 00:57:03.200
Like it felt, he said it felt like cheating.

1344
00:57:03.220 --> 00:57:06.140
So he had to fire someone we'd already seen on the show.

1345
00:57:06.280 --> 00:57:08.340
So we had two stand-ins at the time.

1346
00:57:08.620 --> 00:57:10.600
And maybe we should tell people what a stand-in is.

1347
00:57:10.600 --> 00:57:14.920
A stand-in is a person who fills in for the actor after the rehearsal of a scene while

1348
00:57:14.920 --> 00:57:16.060
the crew adjusts the lights.

1349
00:57:16.260 --> 00:57:19.640
They walk where the actor would walk or sit or stand where the actor would sit or stand

1350
00:57:19.640 --> 00:57:20.220
during the scene.

1351
00:57:20.340 --> 00:57:22.300
And the crew figures out where to put the cameras and the lights.

1352
00:57:22.500 --> 00:57:26.140
And then when it's time for the camera to roll, the actors are called back to the set

1353
00:57:26.140 --> 00:57:27.520
and begin shooting the scene.

1354
00:57:27.720 --> 00:57:28.960
So we had two stand-ins.

1355
00:57:29.160 --> 00:57:30.580
They were Creed and Devin.

1356
00:57:31.020 --> 00:57:35.620
And both Creed and Devin had also appeared on camera in the background of some conference

1357
00:57:35.620 --> 00:57:38.440
room scenes or in the annex by the break room.

1358
00:57:38.440 --> 00:57:42.100
Maybe they would like cross in the deep background of a scene like they were delivering mail.

1359
00:57:42.320 --> 00:57:46.320
I remember we had that mail cart that really made no sense because we didn't have a mail

1360
00:57:46.320 --> 00:57:47.580
room, but whatever.

1361
00:57:47.800 --> 00:57:48.920
But they'd never spoken.

1362
00:57:49.140 --> 00:57:49.880
They'd been established.

1363
00:57:49.880 --> 00:57:50.980
So that was enough for Greg.

1364
00:57:51.300 --> 00:57:58.140
But then Greg, who is the gentlest man, had the dilemma of picking who to fire, Creed

1365
00:57:58.140 --> 00:57:59.420
or Devin.

1366
00:57:59.560 --> 00:58:05.120
I can't imagine how that weighed on Greg because he really is just a kind, kind-hearted

1367
00:58:05.120 --> 00:58:08.620
person and it just must have wrecked him.

1368
00:58:09.040 --> 00:58:16.060
So Greg spoke with both of them and he found out that Devin had just been cast in an off-Broadway

1369
00:58:16.060 --> 00:58:17.080
play in New York.

1370
00:58:17.200 --> 00:58:19.520
So this was all Greg needed to hear.

1371
00:58:19.640 --> 00:58:22.980
He decided Devin would be fired since he had this job to go to.

1372
00:58:23.020 --> 00:58:23.480
He had something to go to.

1373
00:58:23.480 --> 00:58:30.040
And Creed went from being a non-speaking stand-in in our show to getting a five-page scene with

1374
00:58:30.040 --> 00:58:30.680
Steve Carell.

1375
00:58:30.940 --> 00:58:31.660
No audition.

1376
00:58:32.320 --> 00:58:33.580
Greg had never seen him act.

1377
00:58:33.780 --> 00:58:35.300
Handed him a five-page scene with Steve Carell.

1378
00:58:35.460 --> 00:58:41.200
The scene was so popular with fans and Greg was so delighted that he started writing more

1379
00:58:41.200 --> 00:58:44.800
stuff for Creed and then Creed eventually also became a series regular.

1380
00:58:45.060 --> 00:58:45.280
Yes.

1381
00:58:45.540 --> 00:58:47.260
And it was all because Greg needed someone to fire.

1382
00:58:47.260 --> 00:58:53.920
So in addition to this being the episode that launched Creed into the office family,

1383
00:58:54.140 --> 00:58:56.360
it is responsible for a number of firsts.

1384
00:58:56.420 --> 00:58:59.840
This is the episode that features Jim and Pam's first air high five.

1385
00:59:00.440 --> 00:59:01.660
Aw, Jim.

1386
00:59:01.940 --> 00:59:03.100
Jim air high five.

1387
00:59:03.220 --> 00:59:04.240
Jim air high five.

1388
00:59:04.460 --> 00:59:10.540
It is also the first time Pam is seen wearing her hair down rather than half up, half down,

1389
00:59:10.800 --> 00:59:12.000
which I call the Pam mullet.

1390
00:59:12.120 --> 00:59:16.100
And it was a huge debate about whether or not this hair change could be allowed.

1391
00:59:16.100 --> 00:59:19.400
It went several rounds with the producers and writers before being approved.

1392
00:59:19.820 --> 00:59:23.080
Would Pam look too cute if she wore her hair down?

1393
00:59:23.300 --> 00:59:24.500
They decided to go for it.

1394
00:59:24.680 --> 00:59:28.940
And then the next time you see a hair change for Pam is casino night.

1395
00:59:29.100 --> 00:59:33.080
I mean, if we know a lot of details about hair and stuff like that is because these

1396
00:59:33.080 --> 00:59:34.740
were huge discussions.

1397
00:59:35.020 --> 00:59:39.780
We just always wanted to stay true to the integrity of who these people were and not

1398
00:59:39.780 --> 00:59:43.860
as the success of the show grew, like, zhuzh them up too much.

1399
00:59:44.040 --> 00:59:49.260
They were still in Scranton in a paper company, even though the show was gaining more popularity

1400
00:59:49.260 --> 00:59:54.560
and we were sort of becoming a little bit, like, I don't know, like, fancier.

1401
00:59:54.800 --> 00:59:57.380
Well, this was true of wardrobe as well.

1402
00:59:57.540 --> 01:00:02.500
They only shopped at places that our characters could realistically afford to shop at.

1403
01:00:02.720 --> 01:00:07.000
So if Pam had to wear a pair of jeans, I wore a pair of jeans from the Gap.

1404
01:00:07.080 --> 01:00:09.880
I did not wear a pair of $200 designer jeans.

1405
01:00:10.080 --> 01:00:10.360
Right.

1406
01:00:10.520 --> 01:00:12.580
And also, our wardrobe department was amazing.

1407
01:00:12.580 --> 01:00:15.580
And, you know, they made sure, like, your pants weren't too long or things like that.

1408
01:00:15.680 --> 01:00:19.820
But they did not fit you, like, alter things the way most TV shows do.

1409
01:00:19.920 --> 01:00:23.220
And after The Office, I worked on a show called Your Family or Mine.

1410
01:00:23.440 --> 01:00:26.680
And the women that did the wardrobe also did the wardrobe for Friends.

1411
01:00:27.160 --> 01:00:28.980
And those women looked amazing on Friends.

1412
01:00:29.140 --> 01:00:33.940
And I have never looked so kabam, like, ever in my life.

1413
01:00:34.180 --> 01:00:35.940
They altered it, everything.

1414
01:00:35.940 --> 01:00:42.860
They altered, like, where your shoulder hem is and where, like, the ends, like, everything fit me like a glove.

1415
01:00:43.000 --> 01:00:46.120
Yes, most shows tailor clothes to your body.

1416
01:00:46.240 --> 01:00:46.420
Yeah.

1417
01:00:46.760 --> 01:00:48.720
And that's why everybody looks so fabulous on TV.

1418
01:00:48.940 --> 01:00:52.560
They said to me, do you know what's the difference between just a normal person walking down the street

1419
01:00:52.560 --> 01:00:54.240
and someone walking down the street on a TV show?

1420
01:00:54.400 --> 01:00:55.220
And I said, what?

1421
01:00:55.320 --> 01:00:56.420
And they said, alterations.

1422
01:00:57.000 --> 01:00:58.360
It's 100% true.

1423
01:00:58.580 --> 01:01:02.000
Because most people, like, figure out how to blow out their hair and put a little bit of makeup on.

1424
01:01:02.120 --> 01:01:03.800
But having clothes fit you like that.

1425
01:01:05.940 --> 01:01:08.200
Because I was like, oh, my gosh, look how this fits me.

1426
01:01:08.380 --> 01:01:09.480
Oh, you're going to take that in?

1427
01:01:09.560 --> 01:01:10.180
You're going to sew this?

1428
01:01:10.320 --> 01:01:13.000
And they're like, Angela, have you never had your clothes altered on The Office?

1429
01:01:13.320 --> 01:01:14.800
I was like, eh, no.

1430
01:01:15.020 --> 01:01:15.320
No.

1431
01:01:15.520 --> 01:01:19.300
Because we had to look realistic for that world.

1432
01:01:23.960 --> 01:01:28.320
Another thing about Halloween is people dress up as our office characters for Halloween.

1433
01:01:28.520 --> 01:01:29.160
On Halloween.

1434
01:01:29.160 --> 01:01:30.340
Which I love so much.

1435
01:01:30.340 --> 01:01:31.380
Which is crazy, right?

1436
01:01:31.500 --> 01:01:34.140
When I get tagged as White and Angela, I love it so much.

1437
01:01:34.140 --> 01:01:35.640
You are a Halloween costume.

1438
01:01:35.640 --> 01:01:41.720
So, I also want to say that of all the photos that I get, I think that the most popular,

1439
01:01:41.880 --> 01:01:44.820
the one I see the most, is the Pam Cat Three-Hole Punch Gym.

1440
01:01:45.220 --> 01:01:45.640
Mm-hmm.

1441
01:01:45.880 --> 01:01:46.100
Yeah.

1442
01:01:46.480 --> 01:01:47.100
Those are the ones.

1443
01:01:47.160 --> 01:01:48.060
That's the one I get the moSt.

1444
01:01:48.260 --> 01:01:53.220
I get just myself in an Argyle sweater with usually the girls have their hair in a headband

1445
01:01:53.220 --> 01:01:54.800
or a slick-backed pony.

1446
01:01:55.020 --> 01:01:57.160
And they're all holding a cat-stuffed animal.

1447
01:01:58.200 --> 01:02:00.900
I get a lot where girls are wearing the white cats.

1448
01:02:05.520 --> 01:02:10.560
Another first, this Halloween episode was the first time since our pilot that the creators

1449
01:02:10.560 --> 01:02:14.500
of the original British series of The Office, Stephen Merchant and Ricky Gervais, visited

1450
01:02:14.500 --> 01:02:15.000
our set.

1451
01:02:15.640 --> 01:02:16.080
Yes.

1452
01:02:16.220 --> 01:02:17.860
They stayed almost an entire day.

1453
01:02:18.040 --> 01:02:20.400
One day when we were filming, we were all geeking out.

1454
01:02:20.720 --> 01:02:25.160
Before they left, I asked for a photo with them and some of the American producers of the

1455
01:02:25.160 --> 01:02:25.420
show.

1456
01:02:25.460 --> 01:02:29.700
But I am the only person in the photo who is obviously dressed in a Halloween costume.

1457
01:02:29.880 --> 01:02:30.440
Do you have it?

1458
01:02:31.160 --> 01:02:34.640
You can kind of see, like, Jim has, like, one circle of his three-hole punch.

1459
01:02:34.720 --> 01:02:34.960
Yes.

1460
01:02:35.140 --> 01:02:38.820
When I look at the photo, I like to imagine that I was doing, like, a school play about

1461
01:02:38.820 --> 01:02:42.380
a cat and all these people came to see me and I posed for a picture backstage.

1462
01:02:42.640 --> 01:02:44.200
Yeah, that's backstage at your cat play.

1463
01:02:44.220 --> 01:02:44.820
At my cat play.

1464
01:02:44.980 --> 01:02:45.140
Yeah.

1465
01:02:45.960 --> 01:02:48.420
Here's my one story about Ricky Gervais.

1466
01:02:48.780 --> 01:02:50.060
So, they were on set.

1467
01:02:50.060 --> 01:02:51.460
We were all kind of nervous about it.

1468
01:02:51.540 --> 01:02:53.840
And I hadn't really been in scenes yet.

1469
01:02:54.820 --> 01:02:58.420
But after lunch, I had a talking hedge.

1470
01:02:58.420 --> 01:03:02.980
And I come through the lunch area and I see Ricky Gervais and I'm introduced to him and

1471
01:03:02.980 --> 01:03:06.720
they're like, Ricky's actually going back to Video Village now because he's going to watch

1472
01:03:06.720 --> 01:03:09.160
everything from lunch on to the end of the day.

1473
01:03:09.380 --> 01:03:11.140
And you were the first thing you started watching?

1474
01:03:11.320 --> 01:03:11.370
Yeah.

1475
01:03:11.370 --> 01:03:12.760
And I was like, great.

1476
01:03:13.000 --> 01:03:14.360
And they were like, Angela, you're first up.

1477
01:03:14.360 --> 01:03:16.000
And I said, oh, fantastic.

1478
01:03:16.200 --> 01:03:20.580
And I looked to Ricky Gervais and he goes, don't worry, I don't tsk too loudly.

1479
01:03:22.520 --> 01:03:23.980
And that's what he said to me.

1480
01:03:24.200 --> 01:03:25.080
That's amazing.

1481
01:03:25.180 --> 01:03:25.600
I know.

1482
01:03:30.640 --> 01:03:35.940
All of these reasons, I love this first Halloween episode, but I especially love the end.

1483
01:03:36.080 --> 01:03:39.760
And I don't know if you remember the end, but at the end, Michael hands out candy to

1484
01:03:39.760 --> 01:03:41.360
little kids and I cry every time.

1485
01:03:41.540 --> 01:03:42.880
I'm getting choked up thinking about it.

1486
01:03:43.000 --> 01:03:47.220
I remember there was a big debate over how that final moment should be played out.

1487
01:03:49.060 --> 01:03:53.680
Not the kids should be nice to Michael or if they should be annoyed by him because he

1488
01:03:53.680 --> 01:03:56.020
was going to be cracking jokes while he handed out candy.

1489
01:03:56.360 --> 01:04:01.520
And the writers and producers also discussed if Steve should be happy or sad while handing

1490
01:04:01.520 --> 01:04:02.040
out the candy.

1491
01:04:02.340 --> 01:04:06.840
It was decided that they would start on a spy shot of Michael looking sad and lonely inside

1492
01:04:06.840 --> 01:04:08.840
of his house alone on Halloween night.

1493
01:04:09.360 --> 01:04:10.680
He's just fired someone.

1494
01:04:10.960 --> 01:04:12.160
Then you hear the doorbell.

1495
01:04:12.300 --> 01:04:15.100
And when he gets to the door and sees the kids, he lights up.

1496
01:04:15.100 --> 01:04:20.620
He makes his silly jokes and the kids just laugh and laugh and are delighted by him.

1497
01:04:20.740 --> 01:04:21.580
And he makes this connection.

1498
01:04:21.700 --> 01:04:22.140
I'm crying.

1499
01:04:22.380 --> 01:04:22.540
Yeah.

1500
01:04:22.980 --> 01:04:25.580
And it's just beautiful and kind and heartbreaking.

1501
01:04:26.080 --> 01:04:28.960
And it's moments like this that make the office so special.

1502
01:04:28.960 --> 01:04:32.860
We can spend an entire episode laughing at Michael Scott or finding him annoying, but

1503
01:04:32.860 --> 01:04:37.300
in the end, he will break your heart with his kindness and vulnerability and his desire

1504
01:04:37.300 --> 01:04:41.980
to have what we all want, which is just to love and be loved in return.

1505
01:04:41.980 --> 01:04:48.360
Yes, that is heartbreaking and so sweet and so real, isn't it?

1506
01:04:48.460 --> 01:04:50.640
It's just like such an honest moment.

1507
01:04:50.800 --> 01:04:56.960
And I think that's what I think Greg Daniels was so great at in our show is finding these

1508
01:04:56.960 --> 01:04:59.580
just real life moments that were so honeSt.

1509
01:04:59.660 --> 01:05:03.440
And you've got to know firing someone was so hard for Michael because all he wanted to

1510
01:05:03.440 --> 01:05:04.300
do was be liked.

1511
01:05:04.520 --> 01:05:08.220
And this dysfunctional office family was truly his family.

1512
01:05:08.220 --> 01:05:14.560
It was such a great choice to have him go home and you see why he's so needy at work.

1513
01:05:14.720 --> 01:05:15.200
Yes.

1514
01:05:15.420 --> 01:05:16.600
You know, he's so lonely.

1515
01:05:16.800 --> 01:05:17.680
He's just so lonely.

1516
01:05:17.860 --> 01:05:20.240
And I loved that the kids delighted in him.

1517
01:05:20.420 --> 01:05:23.240
And there's this thing that Steve did with his face.

1518
01:05:23.620 --> 01:05:26.100
He just looked like lit up inside.

1519
01:05:26.100 --> 01:05:30.820
You know, when they laughed, you saw that joy come over Michael.

1520
01:05:31.200 --> 01:05:32.360
Oh, it was so sweet.

1521
01:05:32.360 --> 01:05:39.100
If we ever get to do a reunion episode, I just need to see Michael Scott with his kid.

1522
01:05:39.380 --> 01:05:39.820
Yes.

1523
01:05:40.040 --> 01:05:41.480
And he's the best dad.

1524
01:05:41.800 --> 01:05:42.240
The best dad.

1525
01:05:42.320 --> 01:05:42.560
Yes.

1526
01:05:42.640 --> 01:05:46.460
And his kid just thinks he is everything.

1527
01:05:46.660 --> 01:05:46.800
Yeah.

1528
01:05:47.380 --> 01:05:47.820
Exactly.

1529
01:05:48.000 --> 01:05:49.460
And him and Holly are just happy.

1530
01:05:49.620 --> 01:05:49.820
Yeah.

1531
01:05:49.920 --> 01:05:50.840
It would make me so happy.

1532
01:05:50.920 --> 01:05:51.160
I would cry.

1533
01:05:51.160 --> 01:05:54.260
And maybe he still annoys people at work, but he's like the best dad.

1534
01:05:54.500 --> 01:05:55.100
The best dad.

1535
01:05:55.280 --> 01:05:57.760
And I think it hit me even harder now that I have kids.

1536
01:05:58.100 --> 01:05:58.480
Yeah.

1537
01:05:58.480 --> 01:06:01.180
Because I just know what kids bring to your life.

1538
01:06:05.880 --> 01:06:09.820
The episode was written by resident genius, Greg Daniels.

1539
01:06:09.980 --> 01:06:12.480
It was directed by, do you know, Paul Feig.

1540
01:06:12.680 --> 01:06:14.000
Oh, Paul Feig.

1541
01:06:14.060 --> 01:06:18.280
Well, you know, I was going to guess Ken Quapis or Paul Feig because they're in the early years.

1542
01:06:18.380 --> 01:06:19.060
That's a good guess.

1543
01:06:19.280 --> 01:06:19.460
Yeah.

1544
01:06:19.600 --> 01:06:23.400
And they also, we gave them a lot of the big like milestone episodes.

1545
01:06:24.320 --> 01:06:27.820
During his time on the show, I'm going to give you some fast facts about Paul Feig.

1546
01:06:27.820 --> 01:06:33.480
He directed 14 episodes of The Office, but he also spent an entire season where he was

1547
01:06:33.480 --> 01:06:34.500
a consulting producer.

1548
01:06:34.780 --> 01:06:35.120
That's right.

1549
01:06:35.260 --> 01:06:36.700
And he was there every day on set.

1550
01:06:36.860 --> 01:06:38.940
When he left, I was like, no, Paul, don't.

1551
01:06:39.020 --> 01:06:40.660
I felt so safe with him there.

1552
01:06:40.740 --> 01:06:41.320
It was a dream.

1553
01:06:41.520 --> 01:06:48.840
He also, I think, hosted the most cast viewing parties of anyone in his home with his incredible

1554
01:06:48.840 --> 01:06:49.400
wife, Lori.

1555
01:06:49.680 --> 01:06:49.960
Yes.

1556
01:06:50.060 --> 01:06:51.200
And their home was beautiful.

1557
01:06:51.380 --> 01:06:51.620
Beautiful.

1558
01:06:52.000 --> 01:06:53.600
And just so warm and inviting.

1559
01:06:53.600 --> 01:06:54.280
I loved it.

1560
01:06:54.280 --> 01:06:59.780
They put out a little like snackity snack in the kitchen, and then we would all get our

1561
01:06:59.780 --> 01:07:01.660
stuff and we would go watch the show together.

1562
01:07:01.860 --> 01:07:03.640
We did that so much.

1563
01:07:03.760 --> 01:07:04.080
So much.

1564
01:07:04.200 --> 01:07:04.780
And Paul-

1565
01:07:04.780 --> 01:07:05.640
So many viewing parties.

1566
01:07:05.820 --> 01:07:07.780
Paul and Lori are just such great party hosts.

1567
01:07:07.940 --> 01:07:11.700
Paul is one of the best dressed people you will ever meet in your life.

1568
01:07:11.860 --> 01:07:12.680
Full suit with Ty.

1569
01:07:12.800 --> 01:07:14.320
I've never not seen him in a suit.

1570
01:07:14.440 --> 01:07:17.340
And his Instagram is all of him in suits with cocktails.

1571
01:07:17.520 --> 01:07:19.900
You have to see it because it's very enjoyable.

1572
01:07:20.080 --> 01:07:20.680
It's very impressive.

1573
01:07:20.820 --> 01:07:22.620
Paul, we're about to deep dive on your feet on Instagram.

1574
01:07:22.620 --> 01:07:23.480
Don't get freaked out.

1575
01:07:24.780 --> 01:07:29.040
Also, did you talk about what the crew would do on Friday when it was Paul Feig's episode?

1576
01:07:29.380 --> 01:07:30.480
They would all wear a tie.

1577
01:07:31.000 --> 01:07:31.780
Oh, yes.

1578
01:07:32.140 --> 01:07:32.360
Yeah.

1579
01:07:32.500 --> 01:07:35.920
It would be Paul Feig Day, suit day, every Friday when Paul Feig directed.

1580
01:07:36.220 --> 01:07:39.420
And all the crew members, the camera guys, they would all wear ties.

1581
01:07:43.530 --> 01:07:45.570
Halloween did not end with our Halloween episodes.

1582
01:07:45.830 --> 01:07:51.050
In order to edit the episodes in time to air during the actual week of Halloween, we filmed

1583
01:07:51.050 --> 01:07:54.410
our Halloween episodes like usually in August, like months before.

1584
01:07:54.410 --> 01:07:56.770
So every year we got two Halloween celebrations.

1585
01:07:57.050 --> 01:08:00.670
First was the week that we shot the Halloween episode, but then there was actual Halloween.

1586
01:08:00.870 --> 01:08:04.570
And during actual Halloween week, our crew would all dress up and we would have a costume

1587
01:08:04.570 --> 01:08:05.330
parade at lunch.

1588
01:08:05.890 --> 01:08:07.550
And the crew went all out.

1589
01:08:07.730 --> 01:08:07.950
Yeah.

1590
01:08:08.110 --> 01:08:11.430
We would shoot a whole day with them all in costumes and us dressed as our characters.

1591
01:08:11.710 --> 01:08:15.970
One year, I don't know if you remember this, the wardrobe department dressed as the characters

1592
01:08:15.970 --> 01:08:16.630
from our show.

1593
01:08:16.830 --> 01:08:20.230
And I have pictures of them dressed as the characters.

1594
01:08:20.290 --> 01:08:21.330
That is hilarious.

1595
01:08:21.650 --> 01:08:22.870
Although this guy was a props guy.

1596
01:08:23.010 --> 01:08:25.870
He wasn't wardrobe, but wardrobe dressed as us.

1597
01:08:25.990 --> 01:08:26.610
Oh my gosh.

1598
01:08:26.610 --> 01:08:27.470
That's amazing.

1599
01:08:27.850 --> 01:08:28.230
I know.

1600
01:08:28.370 --> 01:08:29.010
It was pretty great.

1601
01:08:29.150 --> 01:08:34.070
Because I think something that people say a lot, but was very true for us, and maybe it's

1602
01:08:34.070 --> 01:08:34.710
true for them too.

1603
01:08:34.710 --> 01:08:36.810
We were like one big family.

1604
01:08:37.050 --> 01:08:37.610
We were.

1605
01:08:37.750 --> 01:08:38.450
But not just the caSt.

1606
01:08:38.550 --> 01:08:39.130
It was the crew.

1607
01:08:39.370 --> 01:08:39.590
Yes.

1608
01:08:39.730 --> 01:08:40.770
We looked out for one another.

1609
01:08:40.770 --> 01:08:41.890
We enjoyed being together.

1610
01:08:42.170 --> 01:08:44.430
This is a story that's near and dear to my heart.

1611
01:08:44.670 --> 01:08:50.050
When my son Weston was one years old, I wanted to dress him as a Wimbledon tennis player because

1612
01:08:50.050 --> 01:08:54.690
I had a little shirt from Wimbledon, like a little polo shirt from Wimbledon that had

1613
01:08:54.690 --> 01:08:55.610
a little logo on it.

1614
01:08:55.750 --> 01:08:57.130
And it just seemed like an easy outfit.

1615
01:08:57.290 --> 01:08:58.570
You know, the players just wear all white.

1616
01:08:58.750 --> 01:09:00.130
So I had the shirt.

1617
01:09:00.290 --> 01:09:02.870
I put white shorts, little white sneakers, a little white hat.

1618
01:09:03.150 --> 01:09:07.930
And when Phil Shea, the head of our props department, heard what I was putting together, he

1619
01:09:07.930 --> 01:09:14.850
arranged for someone to make him an actual, tiny, perfectly proportioned tennis racket

1620
01:09:14.850 --> 01:09:15.510
and bag.

1621
01:09:15.630 --> 01:09:16.210
I know.

1622
01:09:16.370 --> 01:09:17.650
A real tennis racket.

1623
01:09:17.790 --> 01:09:18.190
I know.

1624
01:09:18.270 --> 01:09:19.470
And it was so tiny.

1625
01:09:19.750 --> 01:09:19.890
Not plastic.

1626
01:09:20.110 --> 01:09:21.170
It was like out of metal.

1627
01:09:21.690 --> 01:09:23.830
It was literally the cutest thing on the planet.

1628
01:09:24.050 --> 01:09:25.190
It made the whole outfit.

1629
01:09:25.590 --> 01:09:30.870
And then I sewed a little yellow, squishy ball into his pocket.

1630
01:09:31.070 --> 01:09:32.110
So it looked like he had a little tennis ball.

1631
01:09:32.110 --> 01:09:33.070
Ball coming out of his pocket.

1632
01:09:33.150 --> 01:09:33.770
Out of his pocket.

1633
01:09:34.210 --> 01:09:35.150
And it was so sweet.

1634
01:09:35.150 --> 01:09:38.670
I brought Weston into work at lunchtime so that he could be part of the parade.

1635
01:09:38.710 --> 01:09:39.510
And I have a picture of it.

1636
01:09:39.630 --> 01:09:42.510
I have a picture of Weston and Isabel and Lee.

1637
01:09:42.710 --> 01:09:44.210
And Weston's in this little outfit.

1638
01:09:44.370 --> 01:09:45.750
And I was coming across photos.

1639
01:09:45.950 --> 01:09:46.930
And I came across it.

1640
01:09:47.030 --> 01:09:48.710
And I didn't have it in my Halloween photos.

1641
01:09:48.750 --> 01:09:52.070
And I was like, why is Weston dressed as a tiny Wimbledon tennis player?

1642
01:09:52.610 --> 01:09:54.450
And then I remembered it was Halloween.

1643
01:10:00.110 --> 01:10:01.230
Work days were long.

1644
01:10:01.310 --> 01:10:03.510
But they were not as long as a typical television show.

1645
01:10:04.270 --> 01:10:04.450
No.

1646
01:10:04.610 --> 01:10:06.530
We averaged about 12 hours a day.

1647
01:10:06.630 --> 01:10:08.390
And it usually was something like 6 a.m.

1648
01:10:08.450 --> 01:10:09.030
to 6 p.m.

1649
01:10:09.030 --> 01:10:11.310
It was rare to go as late as 7.30.

1650
01:10:11.430 --> 01:10:12.490
But it did happen occasionally.

1651
01:10:12.730 --> 01:10:12.830
Yeah.

1652
01:10:12.970 --> 01:10:15.550
But it never happened on Halloween.

1653
01:10:15.810 --> 01:10:17.350
We were always wrapped early.

1654
01:10:17.930 --> 01:10:18.330
Always.

1655
01:10:18.950 --> 01:10:22.290
And I think it's because so many of our crew members had families with young kids.

1656
01:10:22.450 --> 01:10:26.630
And the producers wanted to make sure that everyone was home in time to celebrate with their families.

1657
01:10:26.830 --> 01:10:27.230
That's right.

1658
01:10:27.450 --> 01:10:30.890
And, you know, Greg himself is a very big family man.

1659
01:10:31.170 --> 01:10:32.970
I think that those things are important.

1660
01:10:33.130 --> 01:10:34.030
They were important to him.

1661
01:10:34.030 --> 01:10:43.250
And I think when you're on a show where your showrunner creator has a family, they just go into it thinking about people's families.

1662
01:10:43.410 --> 01:10:44.550
I always felt that way.

1663
01:10:44.730 --> 01:10:51.790
I felt like they knew when I went to them when I had Isabel and I was going to be bringing her to set.

1664
01:10:51.970 --> 01:10:55.210
I felt like I could go and have a conversation and they would help make it work.

1665
01:10:55.470 --> 01:10:59.710
I know that it was very important to Steve that he'd be at home for dinner with his family every night.

1666
01:10:59.710 --> 01:11:03.370
He would come in first thing in the morning, 5.30, 6 a.m.

1667
01:11:03.830 --> 01:11:06.130
So that he could be finished by 5.30 at night.

1668
01:11:06.310 --> 01:11:14.810
And then some of the people who didn't have kids would stay later and then work to that 6, 6.30 time frame happily, you know, for him.

1669
01:11:15.010 --> 01:11:18.650
In fact, there were a lot of younger people who preferred coming in at 8 a.m.

1670
01:11:18.810 --> 01:11:19.790
and working later.

1671
01:11:19.790 --> 01:11:21.490
They did not want to come in at 5.30.

1672
01:11:21.690 --> 01:11:24.870
But once you have kids, you're awake anyway, you know.

1673
01:11:25.470 --> 01:11:26.850
You don't want to miss bedtime.

1674
01:11:26.850 --> 01:11:28.590
You don't want to miss that dinner and bedtime.

1675
01:11:28.590 --> 01:11:33.590
And it can be made to work if the people who are in charge make it work.

1676
01:11:33.830 --> 01:11:36.010
And it happened on Halloween.

1677
01:11:36.370 --> 01:11:45.270
Also, every year, Matt Sohn, who was one of our camera operators, and then later he became our director of photography and even directed some episodes.

1678
01:11:45.450 --> 01:11:48.810
He would throw a party at his house after work for everyone and their families.

1679
01:11:48.830 --> 01:11:50.930
And he lived in this great neighborhood for trick-or-treating.

1680
01:11:51.290 --> 01:11:55.530
And we'd show up and he would have these little tables that had crafts for the kids.

1681
01:11:55.690 --> 01:11:56.190
Oh, he had.

1682
01:11:56.250 --> 01:11:56.770
It was so cute.

1683
01:11:56.770 --> 01:12:00.010
Their, like, front room of their house had little low tables with little chairs.

1684
01:12:00.350 --> 01:12:00.630
Yes.

1685
01:12:00.690 --> 01:12:02.410
It was like kid paradise over there.

1686
01:12:02.550 --> 01:12:04.130
Little stickers, projects.

1687
01:12:04.130 --> 01:12:05.510
You could make a little pumpkin.

1688
01:12:05.810 --> 01:12:06.050
Uh-huh.

1689
01:12:06.290 --> 01:12:07.230
And then food.

1690
01:12:07.530 --> 01:12:08.330
They'd order pizza.

1691
01:12:08.650 --> 01:12:10.570
And then we'd just go out and canvas the neighborhood.

1692
01:12:10.890 --> 01:12:12.930
And even cast members without kids would come.

1693
01:12:13.010 --> 01:12:15.590
I know BJ Novak used to go over and hand out candy.

1694
01:12:15.830 --> 01:12:15.990
Yeah.

1695
01:12:15.990 --> 01:12:17.430
Because someone had to man the door, right?

1696
01:12:17.550 --> 01:12:17.750
Yeah.

1697
01:12:17.750 --> 01:12:19.850
Like, if everybody leaves, someone has to be there to hand out the candy.

1698
01:12:19.970 --> 01:12:20.930
BJ was like, I'll do it.

1699
01:12:21.070 --> 01:12:24.930
I'll never forget one of the years we went, Isabel was Tinkerbell.

1700
01:12:25.070 --> 01:12:28.590
And she was so excited when she rang the door that people gave her candy.

1701
01:12:28.730 --> 01:12:29.490
She couldn't believe it.

1702
01:12:29.750 --> 01:12:31.190
She was like, what is happening?

1703
01:12:31.710 --> 01:12:33.610
But it was really sweet that we all did that.

1704
01:12:33.810 --> 01:12:39.850
Even though we'd spent all day working together, we still shared these occasions with one another.

1705
01:12:40.050 --> 01:12:40.290
Mm-hmm.

1706
01:12:40.530 --> 01:12:41.550
It was really sweet.

1707
01:12:45.560 --> 01:12:48.260
I had this, like, professor in college.

1708
01:12:48.260 --> 01:12:49.540
I'm going to get really nerdy for a second.

1709
01:12:49.800 --> 01:12:51.840
And he was my creative writing professor.

1710
01:12:51.840 --> 01:12:55.840
And he used to say, you know, what's a pleasure to do is a pleasure to experience.

1711
01:12:56.200 --> 01:13:00.860
So if you're writing and you love what you're writing, I'm going to love reading it.

1712
01:13:00.860 --> 01:13:03.640
And I felt that way about The Office.

1713
01:13:03.860 --> 01:13:07.880
We so loved what we were doing that I think it comes through.

1714
01:13:07.880 --> 01:13:10.040
I think we cared a lot for each other.

1715
01:13:10.340 --> 01:13:12.900
We were very invested in each other's lives.

1716
01:13:13.240 --> 01:13:18.460
And I think now the show still resonates because when you watch that, you feel it.

1717
01:13:18.580 --> 01:13:20.140
We were genuinely connected.

1718
01:13:20.520 --> 01:13:20.820
Yeah.

1719
01:13:20.920 --> 01:13:21.880
And we still are.

1720
01:13:22.040 --> 01:13:22.780
We still are.

1721
01:13:23.140 --> 01:13:23.480
Yeah.

1722
01:13:23.780 --> 01:13:24.440
That was our Halloween.

1723
01:13:24.640 --> 01:13:24.720
BFF.

1724
01:13:24.880 --> 01:13:25.540
Air high five.

1725
01:13:25.660 --> 01:13:26.280
Air high five.

1726
01:13:26.420 --> 01:13:26.680
Boom.

1727
01:13:28.590 --> 01:13:30.410
Well, that was so crazy for me.

1728
01:13:30.570 --> 01:13:30.750
Yeah.

1729
01:13:30.750 --> 01:13:32.310
And you listen to after all these years.

1730
01:13:32.470 --> 01:13:33.250
Isn't that wild?

1731
01:13:33.450 --> 01:13:35.870
I mean, it was your 45th birthday?

1732
01:13:36.530 --> 01:13:36.930
Yeah.

1733
01:13:37.110 --> 01:13:40.230
We had just gone for a hike.

1734
01:13:40.450 --> 01:13:42.570
And then we were having lunch.

1735
01:13:42.670 --> 01:13:43.670
I'm so sorry, you guys.

1736
01:13:43.770 --> 01:13:45.110
I was eating a turkey wrap.

1737
01:13:45.890 --> 01:13:49.930
I apologize because I know you have written in, you don't like us to eat on the podcaSt.

1738
01:13:50.070 --> 01:13:52.310
But I didn't know I was podcasting at the time.

1739
01:13:52.410 --> 01:13:55.290
I just was having an afternoon with my best friend.

1740
01:13:55.690 --> 01:13:57.930
I feel like I talked really faSt.

1741
01:13:58.210 --> 01:13:58.850
You think so?

1742
01:13:59.130 --> 01:13:59.890
A little bit.

1743
01:13:59.890 --> 01:14:04.490
But yeah, but I also, by the way, think it's very funny that I brought a document.

1744
01:14:04.910 --> 01:14:05.510
I know.

1745
01:14:05.830 --> 01:14:08.030
You were so organized already.

1746
01:14:08.330 --> 01:14:08.990
From the beginning.

1747
01:14:09.790 --> 01:14:11.250
And you were so relaxed.

1748
01:14:11.510 --> 01:14:13.110
It's like, we're all there.

1749
01:14:13.290 --> 01:14:15.270
Even from the very beginning, we're all there.

1750
01:14:15.550 --> 01:14:19.570
It is funny to me, though, that even though we were there to talk about Halloween, we did

1751
01:14:19.570 --> 01:14:20.830
have the sidebar banter.

1752
01:14:21.110 --> 01:14:25.030
Like me asking you about the fifth element and you hadn't seen it.

1753
01:14:25.110 --> 01:14:25.770
Have you seen it?

1754
01:14:25.770 --> 01:14:31.270
You know, I think I've seen parts of it, but I would do it as a rewatch.

1755
01:14:31.630 --> 01:14:31.870
Really?

1756
01:14:32.250 --> 01:14:32.970
I would break it down.

1757
01:14:33.030 --> 01:14:34.710
I would if you're interested.

1758
01:14:35.050 --> 01:14:35.270
Yeah.

1759
01:14:35.630 --> 01:14:36.170
I would take it on.

1760
01:14:36.450 --> 01:14:37.170
Oh, my gosh.

1761
01:14:37.270 --> 01:14:39.050
Sam Cassie, have you seen the fifth element?

1762
01:14:39.370 --> 01:14:39.630
Oh, yeah.

1763
01:14:39.730 --> 01:14:40.170
Many times.

1764
01:14:40.450 --> 01:14:41.510
I mean, that's a good one.

1765
01:14:41.610 --> 01:14:42.350
Don't you think, Sam?

1766
01:14:42.350 --> 01:14:44.130
Yeah, I think that'd be a good watch for Jenna.

1767
01:14:44.610 --> 01:14:46.010
Okay, I'll do it.

1768
01:14:46.090 --> 01:14:47.310
It's sci-fi-y, right?

1769
01:14:47.550 --> 01:14:49.670
But doesn't it also have a little...

1770
01:14:49.670 --> 01:14:50.530
It's funny, too.

1771
01:14:50.790 --> 01:14:51.870
